Key B::overlay lookups by the encoded op address

B exposes ops to Perl as zptrtable-encoded addresses, so $$op is the
encoded value. B::Deparse records its pessimisations in B::overlay under
$$op, but get_overlay_object looked them up by the raw address and never
found them: deparsing any "my (...) = @_" died with "unexpected
OP_PADRANGE".

diff --git a/ext/B/B.xs b/ext/B/B.xs
index 96d4a25..8c696b1 100644
--- a/ext/B/B.xs
+++ b/ext/B/B.xs
@@ -124,7 +124,7 @@ get_overlay_object(pTHX_ const OP *o, const char * const name, U32 namelen)
     sv = SvRV(sv);
     if (SvTYPE(sv) != SVt_PVHV)
 	return NULL;
-    key = newSViv(PTR2IV(o));
+    key = newSViv(zptrtable_encode(Perl_xsub_ptrtable, (void*)o));
     he = hv_fetch_ent((HV*)sv, key, 0, 0);
     SvREFCNT_dec(key);
     if (!he)
